Added.

The strongest abstraction is legal reasoning as a dependency graph, not a pile of rules.

A lot of law already behaves like chained logic:

if A is established -> B applies
if B applies -> burden shifts
if C exception is proven -> D consequence is blocked
if E procedural prerequisite fails -> the whole branch dies

Once you model that explicitly, you can do something close to legal satisfiability checking: what facts must be true, who has to prove them, what standard applies, what exceptions exist, and where the chain is weakest.

Then you can compile backward from a business objective:

desired outcome -> required functions -> legal categories -> constraints -> safe harbors/exemptions/options -> operating structure

That is where it becomes commercially useful. Instead of asking only, “Is this allowed?”, you ask:

“What lawful structure makes this achievable?”

The distinction I preserved in Pantheon is important: there is a big difference between legal optimization—using entity structure, classifications, exemptions, elections, licensing boundaries, safe harbors, transaction form, and compliance design—and evasion that depends on concealment, sham arrangements, or violating the law’s substance.

The durable version is the one that survives:

audit + disclosure + substance-over-form review + adversarial scrutiny

That makes the “loophole” less of a trick and more of a legal design-space edge created by the rules themselves.

And yes, precedents become primitives. Strip away names and narrative, extract the operative test, burden shift, exception, or factor set, and then reuse that structure elsewhere.

That is very Pantheon.